+Figure~\ref{fig:some} compares the two accounts of \mention{take some apples} and \mention{take some}. Both keep \mention{some} in one lexical category across uses. In \textit{CGEL}, independent \mention{some} jointly fills determiner and head functions, a \term{fusion of functions} \citep[410--412]{huddleston2002}. Under the D-noun analysis, \mention{some} always heads its own NP, through a \term{nominal} (Nom): a noun with its internal dependents, excluding an external determiner.
