+\subsection{Category and phrase structure}
+
+Lexical categories and syntactic functions cut across one another. An NP can function as determiner, as in \mention{Kim's book}, and a determinative-headed phrase can function as modifier, as in \mention{the many people} \citep{payne2010,pullummiller2022nps}. Including determinatives within Noun preserves this distinction: the phrase headed by \mention{some} changes function between the two expressions in Figure~\ref{fig:some}.
+
+Nounhood and ordinary headedness also need separate support. \textcite{bruening2020nominal} supplies a generative defence of N-headed nominals, drawing on selection and conventionalized expressions. Such arguments support N-headed phrase structure without by themselves assigning determinatives to the noun category. Conversely, including determinatives within Noun leaves fusion available; §\ref{sec:fragment} compares nominal analyses with and without it.
+
+The category assignment also leaves lexical restrictions to be stated. \mention{Every apple} is grammatical, but \ungram{\mention{I'll take every}} isn't an ordinary way to accept apples. Making \mention{every} a noun doesn't give it every use available to \mention{some}. Section~\ref{sec:fragment} states the structural rules and lexical restrictions separately so that their contributions can be assessed.
+
+\subsection{Related analyses}\label{sec:related}
+
+The categorial status of determinatives has long been debated. \textcite[24]{palmer1924} proposed placing them with pronouns. He cited disagreement about their classification and most members' ability to \enquote{be used indifferently as pronouns or as modifiers of nouns}. The D-noun analysis retains the distinction between those categories within a common superordinate category.
+
+Other unifications concern underlying representation. \textcite{postal1966} unifies personal pronouns and articles through an underlying article analysis. \textcite[197--203]{sommerstein1972} reverses the direction, assigning underlying NP structure to the definite article and personal pronouns, with relative-clause structures contributing further descriptive material. These underlying structures don't by themselves place the surface lexical categories in the hierarchy in Table~\ref{tab:rivals}.
+
+\textcite{dechainewiltschko2002} further distinguish pro-DP, pro-$\phi$P and pro-NP, connecting different pronominal projections with distribution and binding. Their proposal makes the structure associated with a pronoun an additional question for comparison.
 
 \textcite[232--235]{lyons1968} supplies a useful methodological distinction: distribution can be compared at different levels of categorization. Two expressions may belong together at one level and differ at a more specific level. His discussion of articles, demonstratives and personal pronouns also emphasizes shared definiteness and deictic contrasts \citep[279]{lyons1968}.
